{
  "assembly_notes": "Batch ice-cream freezer on one vertical axis. The grounded `vessel` part carries the body and the freezing cavity of the active vessel candidate; every candidate exports the same three numbers - mouth height, rim radius and cavity depth - so the lid, mount, dasher and drive fit without a compatibility gate. A `drive_mount` part - a posted bridge yoke, a cast shoulder arch, or a low flat bearing web - is FIXED on the rim and carries a bored bearing sleeve on the axis; the `drive` (hand crank or motor spindle) turns CONTINUOUS in that sleeve and the `dasher` turns CONTINUOUS on the same axis, mimicking the drive so the scrape and the input can never disagree. dasher_blade_count blades are set at even yaws and staggered heights, each reaching to 4 mm from the wall. The `access_lid` seats on the rim and either lifts straight off along the axis or swings on a real rim hinge; its centre bore clears the dasher shaft with a 0.8 mm running fit in both cases, and the lift-off travel stops below the mount bridge. vessel_radius_m, vessel_depth_m and crank_throw_m are honest continuous parameters deriving the cavity, shaft length, blade reach, rim, lid, post height and crank arm; palette, stave seams, bands and vent slots are non-core.",
